{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787222698,"nonce":"H7ez8AM0x32207Gf4gTeNZMRn6A=","message":"Done.\n\n**The scaffolding is gone.** `service/view.go` no longer smuggles the headline through\n`GroupView{Title: DigestHeadline(...), State: \"open\"}`, and the comment admitting it was \"a floor, not\na design\" is deleted with the code it described. A digest is not a Case and no longer borrows a Case's\nview shape: `NotificationView.Digest *DigestView` carries `Count`, `CoveredFrom`, `CoveredTo` — facts,\nnot prose. The view carries facts and the **renderer** writes the sentence, which also serves as\n`RenderedMessage.Fallback`, satisfying the `deliveries_fb_ck` constraint that forced the original hack.\n\n`DigestHeadline` had no callers left and is deleted.\n\n**Layout.** `render/slack/digest.go` renders title, `New cases | Span | From | Up to`, a where-to-look\nline and a `posted`/`continued` footer, dispatched on `v.Digest != nil` *before* the mode so\n`post_root` and `thread_reply` get one layout. The span is documented half-open `[from, to)` and both\nrenderers agree with the migration's `digest_covered_to` = EXCLUSIVE.\n\n**Links: decided, not deferred — nothing is linked, and the card says so.** Two independent reasons,\neither sufficient: `GET /cases`'s `since` is a lower bound only, so `?since=\u003ccovered_from\u003e` over-reports\nunboundedly for any digest but the newest; and membership is a policy's compiled regex evaluated in Go\n(`Policy.Matches`), which has no URL form. The card prints where to look instead.\n\n**Goldens.** Both corpora handled — `digest.golden.json` and `digest_no_span.golden.json` in\n`render/slack` (spanless rows omit the three span keys rather than emitting `0001-01-01`), and the\n`test/harness` corpus verified byte-identical since its views carry no digest.\n\n⚠️ **A regression this ticket caused, found in review and fixed:** `render/webhookjson` still read\n`Group.Title`, so a digest reached a webhook consumer as `[UNKNOWN] alert group (digest)`. It now has\nits own digest arm with the same half-open span semantics, `Envelope.Group` became `omitempty` so a\ndigest declines to assert a group rather than emitting a zeros object, and two webhook goldens pin it.\n\nKnown cosmetic debt, escalated not hidden: the digest bar reuses `#6b6b6b`, shared with `expired`. A\nsixth hue needs a §H.2 row and an owner's ruling; `palette.go` says so at the collision.\n\nSPEC §H.7's action-element limit went 4 → 5 to fit the snooze select — that is ADR 0043, not this\nticket, and an earlier comment here misattributed it.","files":null}]}